Here is the full slice, built on the anonymous-write shape from the form-data-flow golden path. The one contract that must not break: the honeypot field name (`website`) appears in three places — the markup, the JS payload, and the permission callable — and the form **always sends it**, because an absent param arrives as `null`, which is not `''`.

## 1. Route registration — in the owning service's `init()`

```php
<?php
declare(strict_types=1);

use Acme\Modules\Contact\ContactHandler;

$handler = ntdst_get(ContactHandler::class);

ntdst_rest('acme/v1')
    ->post('/contact', [$handler, 'submit'], [
        // ANONYMOUS WRITE — the ONE door is your own callable.
        // A write verb with only a posture does not register (INV-3),
        // and 'public'-the-string is retired. A callable is used as given.
        'permission'  => static function (WP_REST_Request $request): bool {
            // Honeypot: a human never fills this hidden field, but the
            // form ALWAYS sends it. The `?? ''` is load-bearing —
            // get_param() returns null for an absent field, and a bare
            // `=== ''` would then deny every submission that omits it.
            return ($request->get_param('website') ?? '') === '';
        },
        'rate_limit'  => 5,
        'rate_window' => 60,
    ]);
```

The rate limit is charged from inside the permission check (INV-7): a caller who fails the gate never writes a rate-limit row. Note what the honeypot is and is not — spam **deterrence**, not authorization. Anyone who reads the form HTML can pass it, so everything downstream must hold on the assumption an attacker did.

## 2. The handler — thin, `pending` status, no envelope

```php
<?php
declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Acme\Modules\Contact;

use WP_Error;
use WP_REST_Request;
use WP_REST_Response;

final class ContactHandler
{
    public function __construct(private readonly MessageRepository $messages) {}

    public function submit(WP_REST_Request $request): WP_REST_Response|WP_Error
    {
        $message = $this->messages->create([
            'title'       => sanitize_text_field((string) $request->get_param('subject')),
            'content'     => wp_kses_post((string) $request->get_param('body')),
            'email'       => sanitize_email((string) $request->get_param('email')),
            'post_status' => 'pending',   // THE DELTA vs an internal write
        ]);

        if (is_wp_error($message)) {
            return $message; // never swallow a WP_Error
        }

        return new WP_REST_Response(['id' => $message->ID], 201);
    }
}
```

`post_status => 'pending'` matters: `create()` defaults to `'publish'`, so without it an unauthenticated stranger's submission goes live the moment it is accepted. And `email` must be **declared on the model** — an unregistered key is silently dropped from `create()` with only a log line:

```php
ntdst_data()->register('message', [
    'label'       => 'Berichten',
    'meta_prefix' => '_acme_',
    'fields'      => ['email' => ['type' => 'email', 'label' => 'E-mail']],
]);
```

Repository, the only place that touches `ntdst_data()`:

```php
final class MessageRepository
{
    public function create(array $data): \WP_Post|\WP_Error
    {
        return ntdst_data()->get('message')->create($data);
    }
}
```

## 3. The frontend — `wp.apiFetch`, never raw `fetch()`

```html
<!-- Present, hidden from humans. Never type="hidden" (bots skip those). -->
<input type="text" name="website" value="" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off"
       class="acme-hp" aria-hidden="true">
```

```js
// assets/js/contact-form.js
async function submitContactForm(fields) {
    return await wp.apiFetch({
        path: '/acme/v1/contact',
        method: 'POST',
        // `website: ''` is ALWAYS sent — this is the agreement with the
        // PHP permission callable. Omit it and the param arrives as null,
        // null ?? '' === '' still passes… but if a bot-filled value ever
        // leaks in via ...fields, the explicit '' after the spread wins.
        data: { ...fields, website: '' },
    });
}
```

```php
wp_enqueue_script(
    'acme-contact-form',
    plugins_url('assets/js/contact-form.js', __FILE__),
    ['wp-api-fetch'],   // wp.apiFetch sends the wp_rest nonce in X-WP-Nonce
    (string) filemtime($jsFile),
    true,
);
```

## Why the pieces agree

- **Field name** — `website` in the markup, the payload, and `get_param('website')`. Change one, change all three, or every real submission is refused.
- **Nonce** — nothing to fetch: `wp.apiFetch` sends and refreshes the `wp_rest` nonce itself. That is CSRF only (INV-4), never access control — the permission callable is the whole gate.
- **Response shape** — there is no envelope. The client reads `response.id`, not `response.data.id`.
- **Sanitize twice** — the handler sanitizes per param; the model's `email` type sanitizes again on write. Escape is n/a here: the response is JSON, there is no render sink.
